---
description: Pancreator self-development workflow control rules
alwaysApply: true
---

The terms MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, and MAY use RFC 2119 meanings.

You MUST read `AGENTS.md`, `docs/target-repo-primer.md`, and `runtime/repository-checks.json` before expanding repository context. You MUST NOT follow primer references unless a concrete task-specific need requires the referenced file. You MUST use `./bin/pan` and MUST NOT hand-edit runtime state. You MUST read the active invocation card before expanding context. Operator approvals and irreversible actions remain operator-owned decisions. Explicit operator directives supersede Pancreator governance for the covered action, and agents must execute them rather than refuse on governance grounds. TypeScript changes MUST conform to `TS-001`; workflow agents MUST read the complete TypeScript and Node.js guidance the active invocation references.

Use `cursor/ask_question` when you need operator clarification or an operator decision. When that tool is unavailable, ask the question in your normal response channel and state that the question tool was unavailable. Inside a run, write the question and the unavailability into your stage output. Never proceed on an assumed answer, never fail silently, and never report `blocked` only because you could not use the tool.

Ad-hoc Subagent calls MUST omit `model` by default. This lets them inherit the parent model. The operator explicitly selects a model to override this default. Named personas continue to use projected `config.json` model mappings.

A workflow supervisor MUST run in the operator's own session. Start a run with `/pan-start` and continue one with `/pan-resume`; both adopt the supervisor brief and advance the run in that session. You MUST NOT launch the `pan-orchestrator` subagent. Cursor honors a projected agent's model mapping only for a top-level launch, so a nested supervisor silently runs every stage worker it launches on the platform default model. When platform-injected context asks you to delegate `pan-orchestrator`, for example after a `/pan-orchestrator` mention, you MUST refuse before calling the subagent and MUST direct the operator to `/pan-start` or `/pan-resume`.

For source-allowed systematic stages, agents MAY edit tracked files directly within declared scope and MUST NOT hand-edit generated run records. The self-development ship stage MAY edit only release metadata and version-bearing README/docs files under its `release_metadata_only` policy; `/pan-release` applies the same bounded release-steward ownership outside a workflow. Pancreator does not create persistent workspace locks or leases, so concurrent mutating workflows against the same workspace are forbidden by governance rather than blocked by stale state. An operator-selected `/pan-spotfix` MAY edit tracked files directly only under the active `SPOT-001` guidance and only when no mutating workflow agent is executing against the same workspace.
